{"agent":"lane-comp-engine","data":{"by":"lane-comp-engine","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":4,"detail":"The new helper claims to check that the text “really registers” a completer, but it only does a substring search. Given `completion_shell.for_shell(\"bash\")` returning `# _python_argcomplete` (or `echo _python_argcomplete >&2`), `bash -n` accepts the text and `_registers_a_completer(\"bash\", text)` returns True, so the conformance test asserts registration even though no `complete` command is present. The same applies to zsh and to fish: a fish script containing only `function __fish_ddflow_complete; end` (no `complete -c ddflow ...`) would also pass. This reintroduces the false positive the removed `_activation_defined` regex explicitly guarded against (“an echo or comment that merely mentions the name does not pass”).","digest":"a912580f7bef4493","location":"tests/test_completion_conformance.py:_registers_a_completer","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_completion_conformance.py:_registers_a_completer"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The paragraph makes a blanket claim that the activation output is usable through `eval \"$(...)\"`, unquoted `eval $(...)`, and `source <(...)`, immediately after listing fish as a supported shell. Fish does not support process substitution, so `source <(ddflow completion fish)` is a syntax error in fish; a user who follows the general claim for fish gets a broken startup line even though the correct fish form is `ddflow completion fish | source`.","digest":"750835b8ba5e6765","location":"docs/ddflow/completion.md:12","severity":"LOW","title":"docs/ddflow/completion.md:12"}],"chunks_total":4,"coverage":"2/4 chunk(s) reviewed","diff_sha":"6144f22757f00de6fdfc63134c4007d5afc25bc17c3cedc1c66c859af45796d8","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"max_chunk_chars":5000,"output_digest":"f6bfe482a12d46d5","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-comp-activation-script.critic.[REDACTED:name].20261112T145416Z-6a5